    "['ip address unnumbered interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- Juniper uses `unnumbered-address` instead of `unnumbered interface`\\n- `lo0` is Juniper's default Loopback interface name (equivalent to LoopBack1)\\n- The `.1` suffix refers to the unit number (similar to Huawei's interface numbering)\\n- The command is placed under the interface's family inet configuration context\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces Tunnel10 family inet unnumbered-address lo0.1'}]]": 1,
    "['tunnel-protocol [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls interface tunnel-interface.0', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent command for `tunnel-protocol mpls te`:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls interface tunnel-interface.0\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, MPLS TE tunnels are typically configured under `protocols mpls` with the tunnel interface specified. The exact tunnel parameters (like LSP properties) would be configured separately in Juniper's MPLS/RSVP-TE configuration. The tunnel-interface.0 is Juniper's default tunnel interface, though you might need to adjust this based on your specific tunnel implementation.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls interface tunnel-interface.0'}]]": 1,
    "['destination [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols mpls static-label-switched-path lsp-name to 2.2.2.2', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Juniper equivalent for the `destination 2.2.2.2` command:\\n\\n##\\nset protocols mpls static-label-switched-path lsp-name to 2.2.2.2\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the destination parameter for an MPLS LSP is specified using the `to` statement under the static-label-switched-path configuration. The exact context might vary slightly depending on whether this is part of an LSP or tunnel configuration, but this is the typical equivalent for specifying the destination of an MPLS path.\", 'trans_template': 'set protocols mpls static-label-switched-path lsp-name to 2.2.2.2'}]]": 1,
